<code>assert \forall int j; 0<=j && j<=word.length(); this.startsWith(word.substring(0, j));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall String p; this.startsWith(p) == (\old(this.startsWith(p)) || word.startsWith(p));</code>

However, to more precisely reflect the specification that for every prefix of the newly added word, `startsWith` will return true, we need to adjust the assertion to focus on prefixes of the newly added word:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i <= word.length(); this.startsWith(word.substring(0, i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall int j; 0 <= j && j <= word.length(); this.startsWith(word.substring(0, j));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall String p; this.startsWith(p) == (\old(this.startsWith(p)) || this.startsWith(word.substring(0, word.indexOf(p) + p.length())));</code>

However, the above statement is not entirely accurate in capturing the intended meaning. Instead, we need to ensure that for every prefix of the newly added word, `startsWith` will return true after the word is added. Here is the corrected version:

<code>assert \forall int i; 0 <= i && i <= word.length(); this.startsWith(word.substring(0, i));</code>
--------------------
<code>assert \forall String p; this.startsWith(p) == (word.startsWith(p));</code>

However, to ensure that the prefix `p` is indeed a prefix of the added word and not just any arbitrary string, we need to restrict `p` to be a substring of `word` from the start up to some length. Here's the corrected version:

<code>assert \forall int len; 0 <= len && len <= word.length(); this.startsWith(word.substring(0, len));</code>
--------------------
